On Sat, 17 Oct 2026 13:02:37 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> It seems like you just took the three things I did and accepted them. I
> want to know what other people are doing. Thoroughly research and see
> what tools and techniques people are putting into practice for iterative
> development with LLMs. Don't just affirm what I've written.

Fair. I went looking for what people actually run, and for where it
disagrees with the triad. Most of the material is self-report by the
author of the method. The independent evaluations are Boeckeler's [1],
Eberhardt's [2], and two papers on AGENTS.md files [13][14]. I note
where evidence is thin.

Phased spec tools. You write the whole spec up front for each feature.

- Kiro writes requirements.md (stories, with EARS clauses like "WHEN
  <event> THE SYSTEM SHALL <behavior>"), design.md, and tasks.md. [3]
- spec-kit adds a "constitution" of standing principles, loaded on
  every command, then spec.md, plan.md, and tasks.md. [4]
- Tessl makes the spec the source and marks the code "GENERATED FROM
  SPEC - DO NOT EDIT". Boeckeler got different code from the same
  spec on different runs. [1]
- Eberhardt timed spec-kit on one CRUD feature. It made about 2,577
  lines of markdown and took 3.5 hours of review for 689 lines of
  code. Plain prompting took 8 minutes of agent time and 15 of review.
  The generated docs invented rationale, and a bug got through anyway.
  He calls it a "reinvented waterfall". [2]
- OpenSpec is the one tool built for changing existing code. It keeps
  a living spec, and each change is a delta against it, with ADDED,
  MODIFIED, and REMOVED requirements and GIVEN/WHEN/THEN scenarios.
  When the change lands, the delta is merged into the spec. [5] I
  found no independent reports.

Iterative loops. Here the work happens in a small, mutable queue, and
the stable documents stay thin.

- Beck: plan.md is an ordered list of tests. "When I say 'go', find
  the next unmarked test in plan.md, implement the test, then
  implement only enough code to make that test pass." Structural and
  behavioural changes go in separate commits. [6]
- Harper Reed: spec.md, then prompt_plan.md (small steps written as
  prompts), then todo.md, which the agent ticks off. [7]
- Huntley's "Ralph" loop: specs/ has one short file per topic.
  fix_plan.md is a prioritized list that the agent rewrites every pass
  from the gap between specs and code. Each pass starts fresh, does
  the most important item, tests, and commits. He says the plan "may
  be incorrect" and regenerates it when it goes stale. [8]
- Anthropic's harness for long-running agents: an initial session
  writes feature_list.json, with every feature set to "passes": false,
  and a progress file. Each later session reads both and the git log,
  does one feature, and commits. They chose JSON because the model is
  "less likely to inappropriately change or overwrite" it than
  markdown. [9]
- Jesse Vincent's Superpowers: brainstorm a design and get it
  approved, then write a plan of bite-sized tasks, each ending in a
  commit, then execute in batches with review checkpoints. [10]
- Yegge's Beads is a git-backed issue tracker with dependencies,
  built after he had piled up 605 markdown plan files that the agents
  lost track of. [11]

Standing context and scope. These shape behavior across all work.

- Devin playbooks have Outcome, Procedure, Specifications,
  "Forbidden Actions", and "Advice". [12] Osmani splits boundaries
  into Always, Ask first, and Never. [15]
- Gloaguen et al. (ETH, 2026) found that context files did not
  generally raise success and raised cost by over 20%. They helped
  mainly when they recorded non-standard, project-specific practice.
  [13] Lulla et al. found shorter runs at comparable success. [14]
  The two disagree, but both point away from generic overviews.
- Shape Up pitches carry appetite, rabbit holes, and no-gos, and a
  few people feed them to Claude Code. The evidence is early. [16]

What the triad misses, by that survey:

1. A work queue with state. Almost every iterative method has one
   (plan.md, fix_plan.md, todo.md, feature_list.json, Beads). Stories
   say what is wanted. Nothing in the triad says what is next, what is
   done, or what we learned on the way.
2. A check the agent cannot argue with. Beck's tests and Anthropic's
   "passes" flags are both pass or fail. A story's acceptance criteria
   in prose are not.
3. Change as a delta. Stories only add behavior. OpenSpec's MODIFIED
   ("sessions expire after 15 min (Previously: 30)") states a change
   to existing behavior, which is most of what we do to lab-mail.
4. Scope bounds. Non-goals, no-gos, and appetite say what we will not
   build. Beck names "functionality I hadn't asked for" as a warning
   sign. Our constraints are technical rules, not a scope budget.
5. Proportion. Claude Code's docs say to skip the plan "if you could
   describe the diff in one sentence". [17] Boeckeler's main complaint
   is that one process for all sizes turned a bug fix into 16
   acceptance criteria.

Where the triad holds up is the stable layer. Constraints match
spec-kit's constitution, decisions match the design rationale that
Kiro and OpenSpec keep, and stories match their requirements. What it
lacks is the iteration layer (queue, progress, and checks), which is
where the people reporting sustained success spend their effort.

> That's pretty nice. Is status a standard part of ADR? It feels more like
> RFD to me. I'm not very familiar with ADR though.

It is in Nygard's original, which has Title, Context, Decision,
Status, and Consequences. He writes: 'A decision may be "proposed" if
the project stakeholders haven't agreed with it yet, or "accepted"
once it is agreed. If a later ADR changes or reverses a decision, it
may be marked as "deprecated" or "superseded"'. [18] MADR makes status
an optional front-matter field. [19] adr-tools flips the old record to
"superseded" when you write its replacement. [20]

The two statuses answer different questions, though. An ADR's status
says whether the decision still holds, and the record is never edited
after it is accepted; you supersede it. An RFD's state tracks an idea
through prediscussion, ideation, discussion, published, committed, or
abandoned, and the document stays living. [21] So your instinct is
right whenever the status is about progress. On this list, the thread
is the RFD: a patch or RFC is "in discussion" until you apply it.
Only "superseded" would add anything in the repo.

On one file: Nygard and Fowler both say one file per decision. [18]
[22] But arc42 keeps decisions as a list inside one document, [23]
and src/diffwise/decisions.org and src/jc/decisions.org already work
as one file of headings, superseding in prose ("Supersedes the earlier
--help -> help decision"). A single decisions.org, or a decisions
heading in spec.org, loses nothing that matters to us.

> Right now I want to assess the components. I like stories, decisions,
> and constraints. I also wonder if design docs fit in here somewhere? Not
> visual design - but system design.

They do, and they fill a gap. Ubl's account of Google design docs [24]
lists the sections: context and scope ("isn't a requirements doc"),
goals and non-goals, the actual design ("the place to write down the
trade-offs"), alternatives considered, and cross-cutting concerns such
as security and observability. Agents use them too. Kiro's design.md
holds architecture, sequence diagrams, and error handling, [3] and
spec-kit's plan.md requires that "every technology choice has
documented rationale". [4]

A design doc holds the whole shape of the system in one place: the
parts, how data moves between them, and the trade-offs weighed
together. Stories hold behavior one slice at a time, and ADRs hold
one choice each. For lab-mail it would be the page that says Fastmail,
mbsync, Dovecot, and OpenSMTPD sit like this and mail flows this way.
Today that is scattered through spec.org. Ubl also says design docs
drift from the code over time. Nobody I read has solved that, so I
would keep it short and update it in the same series as the code it
describes.

My assessment of the components, for work like lab-mail:

- Constraints, with non-goals added. Keep them short, project-specific,
  and phrased as always, ask first, or never.
- Design. One living page, updated with the code.
- Decisions. One file of headings, and "superseded" as the only status.
- Per change, a story or a delta with one or two concrete examples,
  plus a check the agent can run.
- Per change, a work queue. Your commit plan is the best version of
  this I found; see below.

For a small change, all of it collapses to a commit message.

> Here's another idea I had when thinking through this... what about
> preparing a commit plan before doing the work? Instead of solving the
> problem and then slicing it into commits, produce a series of commits,
> with commit messages and all, that describe the evolution of the
> work. If we can't describe the evolution in a series of commits when
> there's no code changing - how will we be able to do it once we have the
> code?

Others have arrived at it.

- Carlo Gilmar's "Commit Driven Development" (Git Merge 2022): "Write
  the commit plan: Before coding, write down: commit titles, one per
  meaningful change." And: "If something unexpected happens, adjust
  your plan. It's not rigid." [25]
- Arialdo Martini, "Pre-emptive commit comments": "First you write
  it, then you make it true." He checks each commit with "Have I done
  all and only what I committed to?", and notes that the order is
  natural in jj. [26]
- In jj it is plain workflow: make the empty changes with jj new -m,
  insert a forgotten step with jj new -B, and route stray hunks with
  jj absorb. [27] Kurilyak runs Claude on empty jj changes like this
  and says of a separate TODO file: "The file always drifts from
  reality because it's duplicated state". For him, "jj log is the
  todo list". [28]

It also fills the gap the triad leaves: a commit plan is a work queue
whose state is the VCS, which is what "Own the loop" asks for. A
change is done when it is non-empty and builds, not when the agent
says so. And you would review the shape of a series before any code
exists, which is cheaper than reviewing a badly cut series after.

The risk is the one Beck and Fowler raise against specs: you learn
while implementing. Detailed bodies for commits 6 through 10 are
guesses dressed as decisions, and an agent tends to make a message
true rather than report that it is wrong. The Mikado Method [29] is
the counterweight. You try the change, note what breaks as a
prerequisite, revert, and do the prerequisite first. To keep the plan
iterative:

1. Rolling detail. The next two or three changes get full messages.
   Later ones get a subject line only.
2. The plan is the empty jj changes, not a file.
3. When a change turns up a missing prerequisite, stop. Insert it with
   jj new -B and revert the attempt, rather than stretching the
   current change.
4. Before posting, rewrite each message against its actual diff and
   abandon changes that stayed empty, so the series describes the
   patch as it stands.

To try it on the next piece of work, I would post the plan first as
an RFC: subjects for every change, full messages for the first few,
and no code. You would review the cut, then I would fill it in and
post the series. If you want this, name the work and I will start with
the plan.

